{"cut_proposal":{"candidate_errors":[],"prefix":"To solve the problem, we start by using the given information and trigonometric identities. We know that \\(\\alpha + \\beta + \\gamma = \\pi\\), which implies \\(\\gamma = \\pi - (\\alpha + \\beta)\\). Therefore, we can use the identity for the tangent of a sum of angles to express \\(\\tan \\gamma\\):\n\n\\[\n\\tan \\gamma = \\tan (\\pi - (\\alpha + \\beta)) = -\\tan (\\alpha + \\beta) = -\\frac{\\tan \\alpha + \\tan \\beta}{1 - \\tan \\alpha \\tan \\beta}\n\\]\n\nWe are also given that \\(\\tan \\alpha \\tan \\beta = \\csc \\frac{\\pi}{3} = \\csc 60^\\circ = \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\). Let \\(x = \\tan \\alpha\\) and \\(y = \\tan \\beta\\). Then \\(xy = \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\). Using the tangent addition formula, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\tan \\gamma = -\\frac{x + y}{1 - xy} = -\\frac{x + y}{1 - \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}} = -\\frac{x + y}{\\frac{3 - 2\\sqrt{3}}{3}} = -\\frac{3(x + y)}{3 - 2\\sqrt{3}}\n\\]\n\nTo simplify \\(\\frac{\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta}{\\cos \\gamma}\\), we use the identity \\(\\cos \\gamma = -\\cos (\\alpha + \\beta) = -(\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta - \\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta)\\). However, a more straightforward approach is to use the product-to-sum identities and the given information.\n\nWe know that:\n\n\\[\n\\cos \\gamma = -\\cos (\\alpha + \\beta) = -(\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta - \\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta)\n\\]\n\n","prefix_end":1244,"status":"review_required"},"eligible":true,"generation":{"generation":{"max_new_tokens":2048,"temperature":0.7,"top_k":0,"top_p":1.0},"ids":[1249,11625,279,3491,11,582,1191,553,1667,279,2661,1995,323,52933,263,23375,39421,13,1205,1414,429,1124,11520,7141,488,1124,19127,488,1124,32214,284,1124,2493,59,701,892,23945,1124,11520,32214,284,1124,2493,481,19788,7141,488,1124,19127,10699,568,15277,11,582,646,990,279,9569,369,279,68660,315,264,2629,315,25941,311,3158,1124,11520,52591,1124,32214,59,7731,59,9640,4955,276,1124,32214,284,1124,52591,19788,249